Every senior backend engineer knows the pain of unneeded database lookups. When running high-throughput distributed databases like Scylla or Cassandra, disk input and output will quickly become your operational ceiling. Bluesky runs millions of checks per second just to determine whether user relationships like blocks or mutes exist, and hitting disk for negative lookups is a waste of scarce hardware capacity.

Traditional Bloom filters rely on k independent hash functions across a bit array to keep false positives low. However, running multiple hashes introduces computational overhead that adds up at scale. By leveraging Kirsch-Mitzenmacher style techniques to derive multiple bit positions from a single 64-bit hash function, teams can achieve the same false positive rate with significantly lower CPU overhead.

At Bluesky, keeping these filters in memory dropped database query volume dramatically, maintaining a 99 percent true negative rate that bypasses the database completely. It is a textbook reminder that before you reach for larger clusters, optimizing your probabilistic data structures can eliminate your query bottlenecks at the edge.

Never pay for a database round-trip when an in-memory bit check can answer the question for you.

