Speculative decoding actually increases the total floating point operations required to generate a sequence, yet it consistently slashes inference latency.

The reason is that standard autoregressive generation is strictly memory-bandwidth bound. Every time a language model emits a single token, the GPU must sweep all model parameters across memory buses. For a 27-billion parameter model, you stream tens of gigabytes across the memory bus just to compute one token. The GPU compute cores spend most of their time idle, waiting for weights to arrive.

Speculative decoding flips this bottleneck using a small draft model. The draft model rapidly suggests multiple candidate tokens, and the target model verifies the entire sequence simultaneously in a single forward pass. Because modern GPUs process multiple tokens in parallel with nearly the exact same memory transfer overhead as one token, the verification phase shifts the workload toward being compute bound.

You trade surplus mathematical operations for vastly superior hardware utilization.

