JEP 546 tackles this trade-off directly by introducing adaptive heap sizing for the Z Garbage Collector (ZGC). ZGC is a concurrent tracing collector where compaction cost depends entirely on the volume of live objects rather than total heap size. By continuously tracking host memory contention and live allocation rates, ZGC can dynamically resize the working heap to match actual application demand and neighbor pressures.

This means production workloads running on shared Kubernetes nodes can achieve the throughput and latency of a manually tuned heap without static memory over-provisioning. The runtime reclaims unused pages back to the operating system when traffic drops, and claims them back safely when demand surges.

